## DESCRIPTION
##   Find a Normal Vector to a Plane
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by nhamblet

## DBsubject(Geometry)
## DBchapter(Vector geometry)
## DBsection(Planes)
## Date(8/23/07)
## Institution(Union College)
## MLT(planes_find_n)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus: Early Transcendentals')
## AuthorText1('Rogawski')
## EditionText1('1')
## Section1('12.5')
## Problem1('11 15')
## KEYWORDS('Vector', 'Plane', 'Normal', 'Perpendicular', 'Intersect')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Vector");

$a = non_zero_random(-5, 5);
$b = non_zero_random(-5, 5);
$c = non_zero_random(-5, 5);

#  The normal vector
$N = Vector($b * $c, $a * $c, $a * $b);

BEGIN_PGML

Let [`P`] be the plane in space that intersects the [`x`]-axis at [`[$a]`],
the [`y`]-axis at [`[$b]`], and the [`z`]-axis at [`[$c]`].  Find a vector
[`\vec{v}`] that is perpendicular to [`P`].

[`\vec{v}`] = [_]{$N->cmp(parallel=>1)}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
If you can find a vector perpendicular to _two_ vectors in the plane, it will be perpendicular to all of them.  Can you find two vectors in this plane?
END_PGML_HINT

Context('Point');

$P1 = Point($a,0,0);
$P2 = Point(0,$b,0);
$P3 = Point(0,0,$c);

$U1 = Vector($P2-$P1);
$U2 = Vector($P3-$P1);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Since we know the intercepts, let defined them as  [`P_1 = [$P1], P_2 = [$P2], P_3=[$P3]`]. Two vectors in the plane are 

    [```
    \vec{u}_1 = \overrightarrow{P_1P_2} = [$U1] \qquad
    \vec{u}_2 = \overrightarrow{P_1P_3} = [$U2] 
    ```]
Finally, using the cross produce to find the normal vector to the plane. 

[```
\vec{u}_1 \times \vec{u}_2 = [$U1] \times [$U2] = [$N]
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
